00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:09.020
Getting Beyond Data Gravity--

00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:10.910
Moving Your Data to Cloud.

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:13.280
I am one of your
co-hosts, Ash Ahluwalia.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:15.920
I'm a product manager for
the Google Cloud platform

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:17.420
and I work on
transfer products that

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:21.117
help you move your
data over the network.

00:00:21.117 --> 00:00:22.200
ABHISHEK LAL: Thanks, Ash.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:22.850
Hi, folks.

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:25.110
I'm Abhishek Lal,
a product manager

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:27.030
with Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:30.060
I'm excited to present this
session today and discuss

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:32.070
moving your data to the cloud.

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:35.580
We will start by discussing
data transfer use cases

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:39.450
and challenges, then move to
some exciting announcements

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:41.200
that we have to share.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:44.920
We will also do a deep dive
into all the new capabilities.

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:48.150
And finally, conclude with
Q&amp;A at the end of the session.

00:00:50.985 --> 00:00:52.360
ASH AHLUWALIA: So
to start, let's

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:55.240
talk a little bit about why we
built this suite of transfer

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:55.870
products.

00:00:55.870 --> 00:00:57.700
And the principal
motivation is that being

00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:01.150
able to move your data is
an important prerequisite

00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:03.310
to getting value out
of your Google Cloud

00:01:03.310 --> 00:01:06.650
investment across a
variety of use cases,

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:10.120
whether you're trying to
lower your costs by archiving

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:12.130
or offloading your
storage to the cloud,

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:13.630
or in situations
where you're trying

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:16.510
to get more value from your
data by running analytical

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:18.400
or machine learning pipelines.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.110
It also applies in
situations where

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:21.777
you're lifting and
shifting the workload

00:01:21.777 --> 00:01:25.040
to cloud as part of a larger
transformation initiative.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:27.190
And in some instances,
it's important to be

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:29.230
able to move your data
to share information

00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:32.230
between organizations
within your company

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:37.015
and with external
partners and customers.

00:01:37.015 --> 00:01:39.140
ABHISHEK LAL: When moving
your data into the cloud,

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:41.990
customers may face
several challenges.

00:01:41.990 --> 00:01:44.020
Scaling your
processes and scripts

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:47.390
to terabytes and even
petabytes is laborious.

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:50.000
Utilizing all of
your resources--

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:53.060
from source IOPS,
network bandwidth,

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:55.880
and then optimizing
tools and API calls--

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:58.310
requires a lot of tuning.

00:01:58.310 --> 00:02:03.650
Validating data integrity across
formats and transfer errors

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:05.090
can be tricky.

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:06.830
And finally, all
the time that you

00:02:06.830 --> 00:02:10.820
spend in building this knowledge
and completing the operations

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:13.040
could be better
spent on something

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:17.000
that has longer-term
sustained business value.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:20.510
Hence, at Google Cloud, we
have several transfer services

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:22.770
to address these challenges.

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:27.063
Next, Ash will provide an
overview of what we offer.

00:02:27.063 --> 00:02:29.480
ASH AHLUWALIA: Let's talk a
bit about the transfer product

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:31.440
offerings that we have today.

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:34.100
And we'll start with the
storage transfer service, which

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:36.920
is a managed service for
large-scale batch data

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:40.280
transfers from one
GCP region to another

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:45.250
or from other clouds like AWS
S3 into Google Cloud Storage.

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:48.020
The second product offering,
the storage transfer service

00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:50.480
for on premises data,
helps you move data

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:53.840
from on premises file systems
into Google Cloud Storage.

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:57.050
That is also a managed service
for large-scale batch data

00:02:57.050 --> 00:02:58.580
transfers.

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:01.010
In some instances, you
need to move your data

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:03.320
from your private
data center to cloud,

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:05.100
and it's just a
small bit of data.

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:09.550
And for that, we offer the
gsutil command-line utility.

00:03:09.550 --> 00:03:11.780
This is a utility you can
invoke inside of a script

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:15.250
or from a shell as part
of your daily workflow.

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:17.640
Now, all of the products
that I've talked about so far

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:19.440
move data over the network.

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:22.608
In some instances,
you have too much data

00:03:22.608 --> 00:03:24.900
to move over the network--
it would just take too long,

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:27.720
or you may have no network
connectivity at all.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:29.700
In those instances,
we can ship you

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:32.070
a physical product called
the Transfer Appliance, which

00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:34.890
you can load with data and
then ship back to Google

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:37.270
for upload to the cloud.

00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:39.600
So let's now talk a little
bit about the new product

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:43.133
announcements and updates that
we've made to these services.

00:03:43.133 --> 00:03:45.300
We'll start with the Storage
Transfer Service, which

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:47.460
moves data between clouds.

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:49.680
We're really excited
today to announce

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:54.000
that we're launching a public
global beta for supporting

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:55.080
Azure blob storage.

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:58.500
This will enable you to move
data from Azure blob storage

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:00.960
to Google Cloud Storage
with all of the features

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:05.940
that we offer for S3 to Google
Cloud Storage transfers today.

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:08.460
In addition, we're
announcing a public beta

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:10.500
for VPC Service Controls.

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:12.330
This will enable
you to move data

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:15.630
from outside of Google Cloud
into a Google Cloud Storage

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:18.360
bucket that is inside
of a security perimeter.

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:21.690
In addition, it will enable
you to move data between two

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:22.650
security perimeters.

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:24.930
If the source bucket
is in one parameter

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:26.700
and the destination
bucket is in another,

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:30.140
it will enable you to
move data between the two.

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:32.840
Finally, we've launched
a number of improvements

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:35.150
for programmatic
usage of the service,

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:38.180
starting with the
customer provided job-IDs.

00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:41.618
This will enable you to set
the transfer ID for transfers

00:04:41.618 --> 00:04:43.910
you're executing so you don't
have to maintain mappings

00:04:43.910 --> 00:04:45.050
to other ID spaces.

00:04:45.050 --> 00:04:46.940
For instance, if you're
running pipelines

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:48.890
that do a lot of
data transfers, you

00:04:48.890 --> 00:04:51.732
can just use your pipeline
run ID as your job ID,

00:04:51.732 --> 00:04:53.690
and that will make
troubleshooting and tracking

00:04:53.690 --> 00:04:55.370
a whole lot easier.

00:04:55.370 --> 00:04:58.790
We're also enabling the
ability to choose objects

00:04:58.790 --> 00:05:01.490
from the source based on
their last modified time.

00:05:01.490 --> 00:05:05.030
So you can also, for instance,
move all objects that

00:05:05.030 --> 00:05:07.310
were last modified in January.

00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:09.620
And finally, we're
announcing the availability

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:11.660
of PubSub notifications.

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:14.030
When a transfer completes,
we will publish a message

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:17.065
to a PubSub topic that you
have configured in advance.

00:05:17.065 --> 00:05:18.440
And you can then
use that message

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:19.860
to trigger downstream actions.

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:21.920
For instance, if you
want to now start

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:24.040
processing the data
that's been transferred,

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:28.377
you can do so programmatically
with that PubSub notification.

00:05:28.377 --> 00:05:30.460
Now, let's talk about the
storage transfer service

00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:32.000
for on premises data.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:35.780
This was announced as entering
public beta late last year

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:37.520
in Cloud Next London,
and we're really

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:40.700
excited to announce today
that we are launching it

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:42.170
into public GA.

00:05:42.170 --> 00:05:44.210
In addition, we
have added support

00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:46.280
for VPC Service
Controls in beta.

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:48.680
This will allow you to move
data from your on premises

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:52.550
file system into Google Cloud
Storage buckets that are inside

00:05:52.550 --> 00:05:53.930
of a security perimeter.

00:05:53.930 --> 00:05:56.450
In addition, this
will make the service

00:05:56.450 --> 00:05:59.660
compatible with scenarios where
security perimeter extends

00:05:59.660 --> 00:06:02.660
beyond cloud and includes
your on premises [? fiber ?]

00:06:02.660 --> 00:06:03.913
resources.

00:06:03.913 --> 00:06:05.330
Now, let me pass
it on to Abhishek

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:08.660
to talk about updates
for Transfer Appliance.

00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:09.950
ABHISHEK LAL: Thank you, Ash.

00:06:09.950 --> 00:06:12.980
I'm excited to announce a
new [? SKU ?] of the Transfer

00:06:12.980 --> 00:06:14.150
Appliance.

00:06:14.150 --> 00:06:18.560
Currently, we have TA100
and TA480 as the two models

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:19.790
available.

00:06:19.790 --> 00:06:23.420
In coming months, we
will roll out the TA300.

00:06:23.420 --> 00:06:26.570
Next, let's understand
each of these new features

00:06:26.570 --> 00:06:30.610
and capabilities in detail.

00:06:30.610 --> 00:06:33.220
ASH AHLUWALIA: So let's start
by doing a quick walkthrough--

00:06:33.220 --> 00:06:34.667
virtual demo, if you will--

00:06:34.667 --> 00:06:36.000
of the storage transfer service.

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:38.040
Moving data from
Azure blob storage

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:40.170
to a Google Cloud
Storage bucket.

00:06:40.170 --> 00:06:42.930
Now, before we do that, let's
just talk a little bit more

00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:45.180
about what this service
is designed to do.

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:47.220
So firstly, it's
designed to be fast.

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:49.890
It uses Google
infrastructure underneath.

00:06:49.890 --> 00:06:52.800
So you're using, for instance,
Google's provision network

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:57.310
pipes from Google data centers
to AWS and Azure data centers.

00:06:57.310 --> 00:07:01.140
In addition, it's designed to
scale to petabytes of data.

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:03.300
And then finally,
we will allocate

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:04.920
a number of workers
behind the scenes

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:06.690
to parallelize
your data transfer.

00:07:06.690 --> 00:07:08.523
And you don't have to
deal with any of that.

00:07:08.523 --> 00:07:10.740
That's all happening
seamlessly behind the scenes

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:14.210
without any management
or intervention from you.

00:07:14.210 --> 00:07:15.730
Secondly, it's
designed to be easy.

00:07:15.730 --> 00:07:17.280
The interface is very simple.

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:19.600
You choose your source
bucket and credentials,

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:22.210
your destination bucket and
schedule, and then hit Go.

00:07:22.210 --> 00:07:24.200
And it starts moving your data.

00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:26.200
And we will talk through
a more detailed example

00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:27.360
in just a little bit.

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:32.890
It also supports moving data
from Azure blob storage, S3,

00:07:32.890 --> 00:07:35.050
and you can also give
it a list of HTTP URLs.

00:07:35.050 --> 00:07:37.510
So any data you can
reach with the browser,

00:07:37.510 --> 00:07:38.985
you can move with this product.

00:07:38.985 --> 00:07:40.360
And finally, it's
compatible with

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:42.200
all GCP-supported languages.

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:44.140
So it should be
easy to integrate

00:07:44.140 --> 00:07:46.830
within your current
architecture.

00:07:46.830 --> 00:07:49.650
And finally, this service
is designed to be secure.

00:07:49.650 --> 00:07:52.690
All connections are encrypted
with TLS encryption.

00:07:52.690 --> 00:07:54.340
It supports IAM,
so you can control

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:58.340
which users can transfer data,
which users can edit transfers.

00:07:58.340 --> 00:08:00.130
And then also supports
data integrity

00:08:00.130 --> 00:08:02.650
by doing checksums of objects
at the source and destination

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:05.110
to make sure that no bytes
have been corrupted in flight.

00:08:05.110 --> 00:08:07.090
And finally, as
we just announced,

00:08:07.090 --> 00:08:11.420
it supports VPC-Service
Controls, now in beta.

00:08:11.420 --> 00:08:13.940
So let's walk through a more
detailed example and demo.

00:08:13.940 --> 00:08:16.910
So if you're going to start
to move data from Azure blob

00:08:16.910 --> 00:08:19.000
Storage to GCS, there's
some initial setup

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:20.530
you need to do for the product.

00:08:20.530 --> 00:08:22.930
As with many Google
Cloud Platform products,

00:08:22.930 --> 00:08:25.330
you need to give
yourself-- your client--

00:08:25.330 --> 00:08:28.000
application access to the
Storage Transfer Service API.

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:30.250
And in addition, you need
to give the Storage Transfer

00:08:30.250 --> 00:08:33.490
Service service account, which
moves data on your behalf,

00:08:33.490 --> 00:08:36.370
access to your source
and destination.

00:08:36.370 --> 00:08:39.440
Now, once you do that, let's
just talk through how does

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:41.270
a transfer actually
work with the service.

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:43.799
There are two key
concepts to understand.

00:08:43.799 --> 00:08:45.140
The first is the job.

00:08:45.140 --> 00:08:48.112
The job essentially
encapsulates the configuration

00:08:48.112 --> 00:08:49.820
for what you want to
do in the transfer--

00:08:49.820 --> 00:08:52.790
the source, destination,
credentials, schedules,

00:08:52.790 --> 00:08:54.933
transfer options, and
that sort of thing.

00:08:54.933 --> 00:08:57.350
And then the second concept
that's important to understand

00:08:57.350 --> 00:08:58.220
is the operation.

00:08:58.220 --> 00:09:02.150
The operation captures a
specific instance or execution

00:09:02.150 --> 00:09:03.210
of a transfer.

00:09:03.210 --> 00:09:05.240
So for instance, you
may configure a job

00:09:05.240 --> 00:09:07.400
to run every day at noon.

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:11.160
And you will have an operation
for Tuesday's run at noon,

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:14.210
which will tell you specifically
what happened on that transfer,

00:09:14.210 --> 00:09:17.910
with counters and errors
and that sort of thing.

00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:20.450
So submitting a
job is essentially

00:09:20.450 --> 00:09:21.560
just a two-step process.

00:09:21.560 --> 00:09:22.860
You submit a config.

00:09:22.860 --> 00:09:24.890
The config will tell
you when this transfer

00:09:24.890 --> 00:09:26.490
should be scheduled to run.

00:09:26.490 --> 00:09:29.180
And then, the service on its
own when the schedule fires

00:09:29.180 --> 00:09:32.090
will create a transfer operation
and execute the transfer

00:09:32.090 --> 00:09:33.865
as you configured it.

00:09:33.865 --> 00:09:36.240
So let's take a look at that
in a little bit more detail.

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:38.660
Here's an example
we have of how you

00:09:38.660 --> 00:09:41.653
might use our REST interface
to submit a new transfer job.

00:09:41.653 --> 00:09:43.320
Now, let's just zoom
into pieces of this

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:45.200
so we can understand
what information you're

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:46.250
sending along.

00:09:46.250 --> 00:09:48.470
We'll start with
information about the source

00:09:48.470 --> 00:09:49.320
and destination.

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:51.590
So as you can see here, you
would supply information

00:09:51.590 --> 00:09:54.680
about your Azure blob
storage account, container,

00:09:54.680 --> 00:09:57.170
and your credentials so we
can access your source data.

00:09:57.170 --> 00:09:59.330
In addition, you would
supply information

00:09:59.330 --> 00:10:04.430
about the destination GCS bucket
where you want your data to go.

00:10:04.430 --> 00:10:08.150
Another part of the
proto is schedules.

00:10:08.150 --> 00:10:10.080
Schedules are comprised
of three parts.

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:11.790
First, when does
this schedule start?

00:10:11.790 --> 00:10:12.650
When does it end?

00:10:12.650 --> 00:10:14.690
And at what time do we execute?

00:10:14.690 --> 00:10:17.750
All storage transfer
service jobs run daily.

00:10:17.750 --> 00:10:19.715
So in this example,
hours is equal to 1

00:10:19.715 --> 00:10:21.710
and minutes is equal
to 1, which means

00:10:21.710 --> 00:10:25.880
this job will run at 1:01 AM.

00:10:25.880 --> 00:10:27.712
And then finally,
you can also submit

00:10:27.712 --> 00:10:29.170
a number of transfer
options, which

00:10:29.170 --> 00:10:31.545
will help you control whether
you're allowed to overwrite

00:10:31.545 --> 00:10:34.490
data for objects that already
exist at the destination,

00:10:34.490 --> 00:10:37.040
or if you want, for
instance, to delete data

00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:38.100
after it's been copied.

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:40.250
So more of a move
operation instead

00:10:40.250 --> 00:10:43.520
of creating a second copy.

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:45.920
And then you can also
submit information

00:10:45.920 --> 00:10:48.140
about what source objects
you want us to select.

00:10:48.140 --> 00:10:49.640
For instance, you
can choose objects

00:10:49.640 --> 00:10:51.980
based on the last
modified time, or you

00:10:51.980 --> 00:10:54.260
can use include and
execute prefixes

00:10:54.260 --> 00:10:56.100
to choose objects
based on their name.

00:10:56.100 --> 00:10:59.910
So for example, you could choose
an include prefix of foo slash,

00:10:59.910 --> 00:11:04.130
and that would mean that we put
all objects whose prefix starts

00:11:04.130 --> 00:11:07.030
with foo slash.

00:11:07.030 --> 00:11:09.830
So after you submit a job,
you're going to get a response.

00:11:09.830 --> 00:11:13.060
And as you can see from this
pretty big response here,

00:11:13.060 --> 00:11:14.560
most of that response
is the config

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:16.365
that you provided
played back to you.

00:11:16.365 --> 00:11:17.740
But there's some
of that response

00:11:17.740 --> 00:11:19.740
that I think is important
to highlight, so let's

00:11:19.740 --> 00:11:20.988
zoom in on that.

00:11:20.988 --> 00:11:22.530
Now, if we take a
look at that, there

00:11:22.530 --> 00:11:24.690
are some standard
metadata you might expect.

00:11:24.690 --> 00:11:26.440
We can tell you when
the job configuration

00:11:26.440 --> 00:11:29.500
resource was created, but we
also include the job name.

00:11:29.500 --> 00:11:33.112
This is a unique identifier
at the project level

00:11:33.112 --> 00:11:35.320
that you can use to make
subsequent calls if you want

00:11:35.320 --> 00:11:37.840
to, for instance, figure
out whether the job is still

00:11:37.840 --> 00:11:39.400
running, what
operations have been

00:11:39.400 --> 00:11:42.663
executed as part of the job.

00:11:42.663 --> 00:11:44.830
So if, for instance, you
want to monitor a transfer,

00:11:44.830 --> 00:11:48.220
let's just look at that example
in a little bit more detail.

00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:50.760
This is, again, showing you
the request that you're making

00:11:50.760 --> 00:11:52.080
and the full response.

00:11:52.080 --> 00:11:53.680
Now, let's just zoom
into pieces of it

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:55.600
so we can understand
it a bit better.

00:11:55.600 --> 00:11:57.300
So first, starting
with the request.

00:11:57.300 --> 00:12:01.170
As I mentioned before, you
submit the project ID and then

00:12:01.170 --> 00:12:04.050
this unique job name, which
will help us identify the job

00:12:04.050 --> 00:12:05.370
resource and respond.

00:12:05.370 --> 00:12:08.220
And the response will contain
the list of operations

00:12:08.220 --> 00:12:11.130
that have been executed
or are executing as part

00:12:11.130 --> 00:12:13.440
of this job configuration.

00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:15.360
Now, if you look at
aspects of the response,

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:16.560
it's an array of operations.

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:19.470
And each operation will contain
some important information.

00:12:19.470 --> 00:12:22.770
It will contain when
this execution began,

00:12:22.770 --> 00:12:25.470
when it completed, and what
status it competed with--

00:12:25.470 --> 00:12:27.240
was it a success or a failure.

00:12:27.240 --> 00:12:29.850
It will also include
error information

00:12:29.850 --> 00:12:32.077
that you might use for
detailed troubleshooting.

00:12:32.077 --> 00:12:33.660
And it will also
include some counters

00:12:33.660 --> 00:12:36.950
that will help you understand
how much data actually made it

00:12:36.950 --> 00:12:40.073
in both byte and in count units.

00:12:40.073 --> 00:12:42.240
So let's look through those
counters in a little bit

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:43.330
more detail.

00:12:43.330 --> 00:12:45.540
So in both byte and
count units, we're

00:12:45.540 --> 00:12:50.070
showing you essentially every
aspect of what the service did.

00:12:50.070 --> 00:12:51.805
How many objects did we find?

00:12:51.805 --> 00:12:53.430
How many objects did
we take action on,

00:12:53.430 --> 00:12:55.770
whether we were copying
them or deleting them

00:12:55.770 --> 00:12:57.390
at the source or destination?

00:12:57.390 --> 00:12:59.940
And then finally, how
many objects or bytes

00:12:59.940 --> 00:13:02.110
did we fail to act on?

00:13:02.110 --> 00:13:05.650
So you can use this for some of
your more detailed monitoring.

00:13:05.650 --> 00:13:07.870
So that was an example
of how we moved data

00:13:07.870 --> 00:13:10.598
from Azure blob storage
to Google Cloud storage.

00:13:10.598 --> 00:13:12.640
Now let's talk about the
storage transfer service

00:13:12.640 --> 00:13:14.350
for on premises
data, which helps

00:13:14.350 --> 00:13:17.860
you move data from private
data centers to Google Cloud.

00:13:17.860 --> 00:13:19.810
So an overview of this service.

00:13:19.810 --> 00:13:22.450
It is designed,
again, to be fast.

00:13:22.450 --> 00:13:25.270
This service requires you to
install a piece of software

00:13:25.270 --> 00:13:28.780
which we call the agent to help
us push data to Google Cloud.

00:13:28.780 --> 00:13:31.660
And we worked a lot to
make that process seamless.

00:13:31.660 --> 00:13:33.910
All you need to do is cut
and paste a few commands,

00:13:33.910 --> 00:13:36.650
and your agent is
installed and ready to go.

00:13:36.650 --> 00:13:38.680
It's also designed to
scale to petabytes of data

00:13:38.680 --> 00:13:40.480
and multiple tens of
gigabits per second,

00:13:40.480 --> 00:13:44.810
so it is very appropriate to
use for large data transfers.

00:13:44.810 --> 00:13:49.550
And then finally, this service
is designed to be scalable.

00:13:49.550 --> 00:13:52.540
So if you want the service to
run faster, all you need to do

00:13:52.540 --> 00:13:55.060
is bring up more agents
and we will push more data

00:13:55.060 --> 00:13:55.790
in parallel.

00:13:55.790 --> 00:13:59.350
And you should be able
to get better speeds.

00:13:59.350 --> 00:14:01.040
Secondly, it's
designed to be easy.

00:14:01.040 --> 00:14:04.030
We try to make the
management and operation

00:14:04.030 --> 00:14:07.610
of this product as simple
and easy as possible for you.

00:14:07.610 --> 00:14:09.992
So for instance, the
agents offer auto updates.

00:14:09.992 --> 00:14:11.450
You don't have to
worry about that.

00:14:11.450 --> 00:14:14.560
It works just like Chrome
auto updates do today.

00:14:14.560 --> 00:14:18.640
It also works on all connection
types that Google supports.

00:14:18.640 --> 00:14:21.670
So whether you're using
the public internet,

00:14:21.670 --> 00:14:23.740
direct interconnect, or
partner interconnect,

00:14:23.740 --> 00:14:25.870
as long as you can
connect to the cloud,

00:14:25.870 --> 00:14:28.340
this product should be
able to move data for you.

00:14:28.340 --> 00:14:30.920
And finally, it's
designed to be secure.

00:14:30.920 --> 00:14:33.640
It uses TLS encryption to
protect your data while it's

00:14:33.640 --> 00:14:36.060
in flight, and also
computes checksums

00:14:36.060 --> 00:14:38.170
at the source and
destination to make sure

00:14:38.170 --> 00:14:40.660
that no bytes have been
corrupted while the transfer is

00:14:40.660 --> 00:14:41.350
occurring.

00:14:41.350 --> 00:14:43.120
And finally, as we
mentioned before,

00:14:43.120 --> 00:14:47.560
it also now supports in public
beta security perimeters.

00:14:47.560 --> 00:14:49.360
So let's talk a little
bit more about what

00:14:49.360 --> 00:14:50.890
the journey looks
like when you're

00:14:50.890 --> 00:14:52.120
trying to use this product.

00:14:52.120 --> 00:14:55.390
So as I mentioned before,
the first step to set it up

00:14:55.390 --> 00:14:57.700
is to download and install
our agent software.

00:14:57.700 --> 00:15:00.620
This software sits inside
of a Docker container image,

00:15:00.620 --> 00:15:03.400
so that container actually
contains more than just

00:15:03.400 --> 00:15:04.990
the binary for the agent.

00:15:04.990 --> 00:15:07.630
It also contains this
auto-updating component

00:15:07.630 --> 00:15:10.030
that we talked about earlier,
and the process monitor.

00:15:10.030 --> 00:15:12.590
So if the agent process
dies within the container,

00:15:12.590 --> 00:15:15.190
we can bounce it back up without
you having to worry about it

00:15:15.190 --> 00:15:18.650
or include that in
your operations.

00:15:18.650 --> 00:15:20.290
And the other thing
I'll just mention

00:15:20.290 --> 00:15:22.990
is because this is inside
of a Docker container,

00:15:22.990 --> 00:15:25.930
we encourage you to use that
with whatever orchestration

00:15:25.930 --> 00:15:26.860
mechanism you have.

00:15:26.860 --> 00:15:29.690
For instance, if you use
Kubernetes to run many agents,

00:15:29.690 --> 00:15:31.780
that'll help us
parallelize your transfer

00:15:31.780 --> 00:15:34.330
and get you faster speeds.

00:15:34.330 --> 00:15:36.090
Once you've done
this one-time setup,

00:15:36.090 --> 00:15:39.280
then running a transfer is as
simple as going into the Google

00:15:39.280 --> 00:15:40.310
Cloud console--

00:15:40.310 --> 00:15:42.123
the GUI for the Google
Cloud platform--

00:15:42.123 --> 00:15:44.290
and submitting a source
directory in the destination

00:15:44.290 --> 00:15:44.802
bucket.

00:15:44.802 --> 00:15:46.510
And once you do that,
the agents will all

00:15:46.510 --> 00:15:49.660
start working in parallel
to move your data across.

00:15:49.660 --> 00:15:52.270
And you can monitor what's
happening with your transfer

00:15:52.270 --> 00:15:53.470
right there in the GUI.

00:15:53.470 --> 00:15:55.810
You can see what errors
have been recorded

00:15:55.810 --> 00:15:58.627
and all the counters that
we talked about earlier.

00:15:58.627 --> 00:16:00.460
In addition, one thing
I'd like to highlight

00:16:00.460 --> 00:16:03.560
is that this agent
fabric is fault-tolerant.

00:16:03.560 --> 00:16:06.190
So if, let's say, you're running
10 agents and five of them

00:16:06.190 --> 00:16:10.150
go down, the transfer
will still keep running.

00:16:10.150 --> 00:16:13.000
And it might run a little bit
slower because fewer agents are

00:16:13.000 --> 00:16:15.850
running, but the transfer
will still make progress.

00:16:15.850 --> 00:16:18.550
If all of the agents go
down, that's OK, too.

00:16:18.550 --> 00:16:20.000
The transfer won't
make progress,

00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:22.610
because there are no agents
available to push data.

00:16:22.610 --> 00:16:24.520
But as soon as
agents come back up,

00:16:24.520 --> 00:16:27.207
all of your pending
transfers will pick up

00:16:27.207 --> 00:16:28.040
where they left off.

00:16:28.040 --> 00:16:30.610
So you don't have to implement
any sort of special recovery

00:16:30.610 --> 00:16:31.750
or retry logic.

00:16:31.750 --> 00:16:34.170
This service has all
of that built in.

00:16:34.170 --> 00:16:35.920
With that, let me pass
it over to Abhishek

00:16:35.920 --> 00:16:38.500
to talk a little bit more about
the new Transfer Appliance

00:16:38.500 --> 00:16:40.830
product that we announced today.

00:16:40.830 --> 00:16:43.250
ABHISHEK LAL: Thank
you so much, Ash.

00:16:43.250 --> 00:16:45.830
I'm excited to introduce
the Google Transfer

00:16:45.830 --> 00:16:48.480
Appliance, TA300.

00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:50.750
We are always
listening to our users

00:16:50.750 --> 00:16:53.480
to understand their
evolving needs.

00:16:53.480 --> 00:16:56.390
And this new appliance
addresses several

00:16:56.390 --> 00:17:00.320
of the features and
requirements you have asked for.

00:17:00.320 --> 00:17:04.250
Next, let me highlight
these asks from customers.

00:17:07.670 --> 00:17:09.920
When migrating
workloads, customers

00:17:09.920 --> 00:17:12.619
want to minimize the
downtime and thus

00:17:12.619 --> 00:17:15.740
reduce impact to their users.

00:17:15.740 --> 00:17:18.829
Fast transfers reduce
the disruptions

00:17:18.829 --> 00:17:22.250
to rhythms of business
when moving applications

00:17:22.250 --> 00:17:23.790
to the cloud.

00:17:23.790 --> 00:17:25.910
Moving large data
volumes can sometimes

00:17:25.910 --> 00:17:30.470
be disruptive to already
stretched network bandwidth.

00:17:30.470 --> 00:17:32.900
And the cost
considerations could

00:17:32.900 --> 00:17:37.260
make it unattractive to go
and procure new circuits.

00:17:37.260 --> 00:17:40.190
Finally, there are remote
and mobile situations,

00:17:40.190 --> 00:17:43.520
like ships, when you need
to collect data locally

00:17:43.520 --> 00:17:46.230
and in a secure and
efficient manner.

00:17:46.230 --> 00:17:48.440
Once you dock at
the port, you want

00:17:48.440 --> 00:17:52.160
to then move this data
quickly to the cloud.

00:17:52.160 --> 00:17:54.840
Learnings from these
users have helped

00:17:54.840 --> 00:17:58.610
to guide the design of the
next generation of transfer

00:17:58.610 --> 00:17:59.300
appliances.

00:18:02.360 --> 00:18:05.390
The new Transfer
Appliance, TA300,

00:18:05.390 --> 00:18:09.320
delivers a simple,
secure, and performant way

00:18:09.320 --> 00:18:12.740
to move your data into
Google Cloud platform.

00:18:12.740 --> 00:18:15.020
This is targeted
at customers who

00:18:15.020 --> 00:18:19.540
are unable to move data online
due to limited bandwidth,

00:18:19.540 --> 00:18:23.120
or desire to reduce the
cost incurred on network

00:18:23.120 --> 00:18:27.980
provisioning, or do not have the
network connectivity available

00:18:27.980 --> 00:18:30.680
in remote and mobile scenarios.

00:18:30.680 --> 00:18:33.010
Next, let's look at
the user experience

00:18:33.010 --> 00:18:38.060
for transferring your
data with the TA300.

00:18:38.060 --> 00:18:42.680
Customers start by submitting an
order form in the Google Cloud

00:18:42.680 --> 00:18:43.880
console.

00:18:43.880 --> 00:18:48.020
We validate your source
location needs and ship out

00:18:48.020 --> 00:18:51.440
the appropriate appliance
for your requirements.

00:18:51.440 --> 00:18:54.080
When you receive the
appliance, inspect it

00:18:54.080 --> 00:18:56.750
for any signs of
damage or tampering.

00:18:56.750 --> 00:18:59.480
A simple attestation
program is used

00:18:59.480 --> 00:19:01.910
to validate that
none of the hardware

00:19:01.910 --> 00:19:06.610
or software within the appliance
was tampered in transit.

00:19:06.610 --> 00:19:10.370
Now, the appliance is
ready for you to use.

00:19:10.370 --> 00:19:12.620
Simply connect it
to your network,

00:19:12.620 --> 00:19:17.430
mount the NFS share
exposed, and copy your data.

00:19:17.430 --> 00:19:19.980
Once all this
copying is completed,

00:19:19.980 --> 00:19:22.320
you can seal the appliance.

00:19:22.320 --> 00:19:26.810
This finalization step protects
the appliance and your data

00:19:26.810 --> 00:19:29.780
from tampering during
transit, and it

00:19:29.780 --> 00:19:34.390
is required before shipping
back your appliance.

00:19:34.390 --> 00:19:37.780
When returned to our
processing facility,

00:19:37.780 --> 00:19:40.690
we attest the integrity
of the appliance

00:19:40.690 --> 00:19:45.310
and move your data securely
into the destination bucket.

00:19:45.310 --> 00:19:47.920
We then inform you of
the successful completion

00:19:47.920 --> 00:19:49.360
of the transfer.

00:19:49.360 --> 00:19:51.280
As simple as that.

00:19:51.280 --> 00:19:54.160
Next, let let's see the
individual capabilities

00:19:54.160 --> 00:19:54.720
in detail.

00:20:00.320 --> 00:20:03.590
The appliance is ready
to use when it applies.

00:20:03.590 --> 00:20:05.960
You remove it from
the case, place it

00:20:05.960 --> 00:20:09.980
on a desk or a rack shelf,
and connect the power.

00:20:09.980 --> 00:20:13.280
All the cables you need
are included in the case.

00:20:13.280 --> 00:20:16.460
The server itself is
designed for performance

00:20:16.460 --> 00:20:18.080
and reliability.

00:20:18.080 --> 00:20:21.290
All SSD drives give
you faster rates.

00:20:21.290 --> 00:20:24.510
Networking can
scale up to 40 GBps.

00:20:24.510 --> 00:20:29.580
And data integrity is maintained
across all the system layers.

00:20:29.580 --> 00:20:34.430
This is a cost-effective service
with a simple usage fee model.

00:20:34.430 --> 00:20:37.130
You can scale up the
capacity of transfers

00:20:37.130 --> 00:20:40.700
by ordering multiple
appliances in parallel.

00:20:40.700 --> 00:20:44.240
This can help you maximize
the usage of your source data

00:20:44.240 --> 00:20:48.680
systems, the internet
speed, as well as optimize

00:20:48.680 --> 00:20:51.600
the end-to-end transfer time.

00:20:51.600 --> 00:20:55.140
Next, let's see how easy it is
to move your data to the cloud

00:20:55.140 --> 00:20:58.610
with the appliance.

00:20:58.610 --> 00:21:01.580
You do not need any
proprietary software

00:21:01.580 --> 00:21:04.100
to move data into the server.

00:21:04.100 --> 00:21:07.970
Just mount the NFS share
from your workstation and use

00:21:07.970 --> 00:21:09.800
any copy tools--

00:21:09.800 --> 00:21:14.210
like robocopy, rsync--
to move your files.

00:21:14.210 --> 00:21:18.890
A single NFS share is exposed
with the entire capacity,

00:21:18.890 --> 00:21:21.890
making it easy for
you to run many copy

00:21:21.890 --> 00:21:24.260
processes in parallel.

00:21:24.260 --> 00:21:28.550
The local command line interface
lets you initialize the server,

00:21:28.550 --> 00:21:33.710
configure IP ports, and check
the status of the appliance.

00:21:33.710 --> 00:21:36.440
The data on the server
is always encrypted

00:21:36.440 --> 00:21:40.970
and we help you all along the
way with shipping, logistics,

00:21:40.970 --> 00:21:43.360
and any required support.

00:21:43.360 --> 00:21:47.320
We are looking to make the
new TA300 available in all

00:21:47.320 --> 00:21:50.380
our main regions,
starting with the US,

00:21:50.380 --> 00:21:54.850
then EU, and followed by
APAC in coming quarters.

00:21:54.850 --> 00:22:01.400
Next, let's tour the security
features built into the TA300.

00:22:01.400 --> 00:22:05.490
Customers care deeply about
the security of their data,

00:22:05.490 --> 00:22:08.720
and we take this shared
responsibility seriously.

00:22:08.720 --> 00:22:12.230
Starting with tamper-evident
tags on the Transfer Appliance

00:22:12.230 --> 00:22:17.240
case to the TPM chip used
to validate the internals,

00:22:17.240 --> 00:22:21.610
every step has been designed
and tested for security.

00:22:21.610 --> 00:22:25.670
The remote attestation process
validates several aspects

00:22:25.670 --> 00:22:29.690
of the appliance after
it has been in transit.

00:22:29.690 --> 00:22:32.510
First, the TPM
module is validated.

00:22:32.510 --> 00:22:35.380
Then, the platform
configuration registers

00:22:35.380 --> 00:22:39.560
are used to check the immutable
root file system and software

00:22:39.560 --> 00:22:43.210
components, such as
the bootloader, OS,

00:22:43.210 --> 00:22:46.850
Google binaries, and
customer-managed public key

00:22:46.850 --> 00:22:48.280
materials.

00:22:48.280 --> 00:22:51.520
This is done both at the
customer site when they receive

00:22:51.520 --> 00:22:55.240
the appliance and at Google
processing centers when

00:22:55.240 --> 00:22:57.280
the appliance is audited.

00:22:57.280 --> 00:22:59.740
Our processes are
built for supporting

00:22:59.740 --> 00:23:04.640
all the key compliance
standards and audited regularly.

00:23:04.640 --> 00:23:09.560
We are there to support you
in all the steps, including

00:23:09.560 --> 00:23:12.650
wiping the appliance
and issuing a NIST 8088

00:23:12.650 --> 00:23:16.070
certificate for that operation.

00:23:16.070 --> 00:23:18.260
We are looking forward
to serving your needs

00:23:18.260 --> 00:23:19.820
with the new offer.

00:23:19.820 --> 00:23:24.638
Next, some important additional
resources and information.

00:23:27.215 --> 00:23:29.852
Thanks so much, Ash,
for the session today.

00:23:29.852 --> 00:23:31.560
ASH AHLUWALIA: I wanted
to thank everyone

00:23:31.560 --> 00:23:33.520
who joined our session today.

00:23:33.520 --> 00:23:35.100
I hope you enjoyed
our discussion

00:23:35.100 --> 00:23:37.830
on the use cases,
challenges, and solution

00:23:37.830 --> 00:23:41.930
that Google offers to enable
you to move your data to cloud.

00:23:41.930 --> 00:23:43.680
ABHISHEK LAL: And we
hope you will try out

00:23:43.680 --> 00:23:46.230
all the exciting new
capabilities that we

00:23:46.230 --> 00:23:47.770
have announced today.

00:23:47.770 --> 00:23:51.890
Thank you so much, and
really appreciate your time.

